    {
      "id": "53813",
      "entry": {
        "title": "ITEM_20170607048",
        "feedback": {},
        "itemBody": "<p>If the yield to maturity on an annual-pay bond is 7.75%, the semiannual bond-equivalent yield is <i>closest</i> to:</p>",
        "properties": {
          "shuffleRules": {
            "choices": {
              "toLock": [],
              "shuffled": false
            }
          },
          "varyPointsByAnswer": false
        },
        "scoringData": {
          "value": "e2916a07-d174-4c9e-9b7f-48fec67fe2ba"
        },
        "answerFeedback": {
          "75bf005b-054b-4d83-9488-d29fc1a6d8df": "<p>Incorrect. The semiannual bond-equivalent yield is incorrectly calculated as [(1 + 0.0775)<sup>2</sup> – 1]/2 = 0.0805 or 8.05%.</p>",
          "e2916a07-d174-4c9e-9b7f-48fec67fe2ba": "<p><strong>Correct.</strong> The most common periodicity for US dollar-denominated bond yields is two&nbsp;because most bonds in the US dollar market make semiannual coupon payments. An annual rate having a periodicity of two is known as a&nbsp;semiannual bond basis yield,&nbsp;or semiannual bond equivalent yield. Therefore, a semiannual bond basis yield is the yield per semiannual period times two. For this example, we need to take the YTM of the annual coupon bond, which is an effective annual rate, and convert it to a semiannual bond equivalent yield:&nbsp;2 × (1.0775<sup>0.5</sup> – 1) = 0.07605 or 7.61%.</p>",
          "f8312f94-ba21-4a78-b101-35227c92bfb1": "<p>Incorrect. The semiannual bond-equivalent yield is incorrectly calculated as [1 + (0.0775/2)]<sup>2</sup> – 1 = 0.079 or 7.90%.</p>"
        },
        "calculatorType": "none",
        "interactionData": {
          "choices": [
